00:23:22.040 --> 00:23:24.089
Uh, I'm gonna be, I'll try to be really brief.

00:23:24.209 --> 00:23:27.410
Uh, I think everybody on the council knows about the work of the National

00:23:27.410 --> 00:23:28.489
Children's Advocacy Center.

00:23:28.810 --> 00:23:30.530
We're founded on two basic concepts.

00:23:30.609 --> 00:23:34.130
Number one, the only way we can be successful with addressing child sexual

00:23:34.130 --> 00:23:36.109
abuse is by having a multidisciplinary team.

00:23:36.510 --> 00:23:39.310
That includes law enforcement, child protective services,

00:23:39.390 --> 00:23:41.430
mental health professionals, medical professionals,

00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:43.489
and that number two, whatever that team does,

00:23:43.569 --> 00:23:44.589
it should actually help kids.

00:23:44.670 --> 00:23:47.170
And Bud Kramer founded this concept back in the 80s.

00:23:47.500 --> 00:23:49.609
Uh, this model has, you know, has grown.

00:23:49.869 --> 00:23:51.530
Uh, we're very appreciative of having our,

00:23:51.589 --> 00:23:54.260
our wonderful campus at the corner of Pratt and Meridian where you can see

00:23:54.260 --> 00:23:56.030
all of our partners that are co-located with us.

00:23:56.109 --> 00:23:59.829
A special note obviously to this council is our integral role that we play

00:23:59.829 --> 00:24:01.910
with Huntsville Police Department Special Victims Unit,

00:24:01.949 --> 00:24:02.829
that entire special.

00:24:02.905 --> 00:24:04.885
The Victims Unit is co-located on our campus,

00:24:05.395 --> 00:24:06.984
uh, and we work together every day.

00:24:07.104 --> 00:24:11.925
We did two forensic interviews today for HPD on cases that they were investigating.

00:24:12.385 --> 00:24:15.625
Uh, we also provide a tremendous amount of training for those officers when

00:24:15.625 --> 00:24:19.285
they come to the unit, uh, and we have a great working relationship.

00:24:19.385 --> 00:24:22.104
You all do have a wonderful Special Victims Unit,

00:24:22.385 --> 00:24:23.824
uh, of, of investigators.

00:24:23.905 --> 00:24:26.104
So let me say that also would, it would be,

00:24:26.145 --> 00:24:29.204
uh, uh, a remiss, remiss for me to not mention.

00:24:29.540 --> 00:24:31.390
That this model that started in Huntsville,

00:24:31.430 --> 00:24:33.709
Alabama has now expanded all over the US.

00:24:34.189 --> 00:24:37.150
There's more than 1000 children's advocacy centers across the United States.

00:24:37.270 --> 00:24:40.650
So once again, Huntsville showing entrepreneurial spirit that has literally

00:24:40.650 --> 00:24:43.229
revolutionized how our nation responds to child abuse,

00:24:43.430 --> 00:24:46.310
and these programs are also operating in more than 40 countries around the

00:24:46.310 --> 00:24:46.810
world.

00:24:47.150 --> 00:24:49.790
Um, but I don't wanna talk to you about child abuse necessarily.

00:24:49.869 --> 00:24:52.619
I wanna talk to you about, I mean, because everybody thinks,

00:24:52.670 --> 00:24:55.469
oh, you address child abuse, but I wanna talk to you about the work,

00:24:55.550 --> 00:24:58.560
the impact of our work in the long term on human rights.

00:24:59.140 --> 00:25:02.540
On school performance and behavior, on economic development,

00:25:02.709 --> 00:25:05.109
on public health, on workforce development,

00:25:05.150 --> 00:25:08.369
and on government efficiency, all in less than 4 minutes.

00:25:09.069 --> 00:25:11.670
Uh, so for human rights, first, let me just ask the question,

00:25:11.750 --> 00:25:14.020
do kids have the right to grow up free from abuse or neglect?

00:25:14.349 --> 00:25:17.300
I believe, every sane person would say yes.

00:25:17.660 --> 00:25:21.020
And secondly, if a child is abused, do they have the right to receive quality

00:25:21.020 --> 00:25:21.650
services?

00:25:21.910 --> 00:25:22.189
Yes.

00:25:22.310 --> 00:25:24.530
So literally in this community, unfortunately,

00:25:24.589 --> 00:25:27.390
children's human rights have been violated whenever they're abused,

00:25:27.630 --> 00:25:30.109
but we as a system must make sure that we're not re-trauma.

00:25:30.645 --> 00:25:33.305
them and so we are addressing human rights.

00:25:33.685 --> 00:25:36.645
Individuals who have experienced child maltreatment are at increased risk

00:25:36.645 --> 00:25:40.074
of educational underachievement, both math and verbal scores,

00:25:40.444 --> 00:25:44.344
and they're more likely to have poor social skills and classroom behavior.

00:25:44.604 --> 00:25:47.765
So if we can intervene on behalf of children when they have these have had

00:25:47.765 --> 00:25:50.925
these incidents and prevent them from having the long term impacts,

00:25:51.084 --> 00:25:51.405
great.

00:25:51.680 --> 00:25:55.270
They're also less likely individuals who experience abuse are less likely

00:25:55.270 --> 00:25:57.520
to have a savings accounts, less likely to own their own car,

00:25:57.619 --> 00:26:00.780
less likely to have any kind of stocks or savings,

00:26:00.910 --> 00:26:02.439
and less likely to own their own home.

00:26:02.569 --> 00:26:07.839
And men who were sexually abused in childhood on average earn 21% less than

00:26:07.839 --> 00:26:09.339
their peers who were not abused.

00:26:09.560 --> 00:26:11.390
And for women it's 15%.

00:26:11.599 --> 00:26:14.640
This is data from individuals who did not receive services.

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:18.280
We're trying to change that so that we increase the economic viability of

00:26:18.280 --> 00:26:20.520
this community by addressing trauma in childhood.

00:26:20.770 --> 00:26:23.770
Additionally, on public health impacts, the adverse childhood research is

00:26:23.770 --> 00:26:26.910
a big study, but across mental health issues,

00:26:27.359 --> 00:26:30.810
and chronic disease, the more adverse childhood experiences we have without

00:26:30.810 --> 00:26:33.010
intervention, we're going to have more issues.

00:26:33.319 --> 00:26:34.479
It also impacts costs.

00:26:34.530 --> 00:26:37.729
9% of all Medicaid costs are associated with child maltreatment.

00:26:38.119 --> 00:26:42.239
Uh, and the cost of health care goes up when we have more health care issues

00:26:42.239 --> 00:26:44.839
for women who were sexually abused it is 16% higher.

00:26:45.119 --> 00:26:47.160
For women who are both physically and sexually abused,

00:26:47.199 --> 00:26:48.579
it's 36% higher.

00:26:48.880 --> 00:26:51.660
So these are numbers that impact the future of this community's health.

00:26:51.920 --> 00:26:53.800
It's also an issue with workforce development.

00:26:53.839 --> 00:26:57.040
The more adverse childhood experiences a child an individual had,

00:26:57.239 --> 00:26:57.959
they're more likely to have.

00:26:58.040 --> 00:27:00.160
Absenteeism on the job, financial problems,

00:27:00.170 --> 00:27:01.160
and job problems.

00:27:01.520 --> 00:27:03.719
So we need to address this in childhood so we don't,

00:27:03.800 --> 00:27:05.699
we have a better workforce moving forward.

00:27:05.800 --> 00:27:11.199
And finally, on government efficiency, we save $1000 by reducing the duplication

00:27:11.199 --> 00:27:13.680
of government activities and having this coordinated approach.

00:27:14.030 --> 00:27:16.589
So it's a smart investment for all these reasons,

00:27:16.640 --> 00:27:17.959
and I'm under 4 minutes.

